---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Track from '../components/Track.astro';
import LiteYouTube from '../components/LiteYouTube.astro';
import { videos } from '../data/videos.js';

const featured = ['B3ItVf-QHZo', 'sYqq8qmViJk', 'QcQqdULskpk']
  .map((id) => videos.find((v) => v.id === id));
---
<Base
  title="Panache — Greenock, 1976"
  description="Panache were a funk, soul and jazz-rock band from Greenock, Scotland, formed in 1976. Their recordings, their story, and the people who made them."
>
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <p class="kicker">Greenock, Scotland · formed 1976</p>
    <h1>Panache</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Five musicians from Inverclyde who set out to become Scotland's Steely Dan,
      held down a residency at the Victorian Carriage, wrote fifteen songs of
      their own, and very nearly got there.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        They played covers to fill a room — <em>Kid Charlemagne</em>, Stevie Wonder,
        Hall and Oates — and originals because they meant to be a band that
        mattered. Tasty arrangements, five-part harmonies, and at the front the
        voice of Pat Hamill: sometimes sweet and soulful, sometimes soaring and
        passionate. By 1978 they had a publisher in London.
      </p>
      <p>
        Almost none of it was ever released. What survived survived on cassette,
        on reel, and on a ghetto blaster left running in a rehearsal room — and
        it is only online now because the band have spent the last few years
        transferring it, tape by tape.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Start here</h2>
    <ul class="tracks">
      {featured.map((video) => <Track video={video} />)}
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:1.75rem"><a href="/music/">All the recordings →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <hr />
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Before Panache</h2>
      <p>
        The band did not begin in 1976. It began in a Greenock classroom, in a
        group called the 2D Sparklers, with a song written out by hand on a sheet
        of foolscap.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="artefact">
      <img
        src="/going-out-of-my-head.jpg"
        alt="A hand-written lyric sheet for 'I Think I'm Going Out Of My Head', with the chorus bracketed in the margin and a credit line at the foot reading: words and music by G. McClarkin, D. Campbell & J. Dunn & John McGeoch."
        width="250"
        height="388"
      />
      <figcaption>
        <strong>“I Think I'm Going Out Of My Head”</strong> — the 2D Sparklers,
        Greenock High School. Credited at the foot to G. McClarkin, D. Campbell,
        J. Dunn and John McGeoch. This is the only photograph or image of any kind
        that survives from the band's original website.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        That last name is the one people recognise. John McGeoch went on to play
        guitar in Magazine, Visage, Siouxsie and the Banshees and Public Image
        Ltd, and is regularly named among the most influential guitarists of his
        generation. Before any of that he was a boy in Greenock in a band called
        Violent Sabbath with two of the men who would form Panache.
        <a href="/story/">The whole story is here →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <LiteYouTube />
</Base>
